WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.155
Five patterns, one question each.

2
00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:05.741
Lesson 1 drew the line between workflows and agents.

3
00:00:06.021 --> 00:00:14.832
This lesson stays on the workflow side, where your code owns the control flow, and works through the five patterns in Anthropic’s “Building effective agents”.

4
00:00:15.112 --> 00:00:22.109
They are worth learning as a set because most production LLM features are one of them, or two of them composed.

5
00:00:22.389 --> 00:00:25.483
Each pattern answers a different question about your task.

6
00:00:25.763 --> 00:00:29.944
Prompt chaining: can the task be split into fixed steps that each get easier?

7
00:00:30.224 --> 00:00:34.149
Routing: do inputs fall into categories that deserve different handling?

8
00:00:34.429 --> 00:00:40.509
Parallelization: are there independent parts, or would several independent attempts raise confidence?

9
00:00:40.789 --> 00:00:46.059
Orchestrator-workers: are the parts real but impossible to know until you see the input?

10
00:00:46.339 --> 00:00:52.504
Evaluator-optimizer: are the quality criteria clear enough that feedback reliably improves a draft?

11
00:00:52.784 --> 00:01:00.592
The running example is the one this studio builds toward: a PM workflow that turns raw meeting notes into a status update.

12
00:01:00.872 --> 00:01:06.141
For each pattern you will see when it fits, a short code sketch, and the check that keeps it honest.

13
00:01:06.421 --> 00:01:08.917
Every sketch uses one small helper, below.

14
00:01:09.197 --> 00:01:17.709
Anthropic’s cookbook has fuller reference notebooks for these patterns (MIT license); the sketches here are written fresh in this course’s style.
